{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"normalized":[{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:US_X_Corps_SSI.png","to":"File:US X Corps SSI.png"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:FORCECOM_SSI.png","to":"File:FORCECOM SSI.png"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:99th_rsc.jpg","to":"File:99th rsc.jpg"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:Flag_of_the_U.S._Army_3rd_Infantry_Division.svg","to":"File:Flag of the U.S. Army 3rd Infantry Division.svg"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:XIICorps_patch.jpg","to":"File:XIICorps patch.jpg"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:USA_-_800_MP_BDE.svg","to":"File:USA - 800 MP BDE.svg"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:Shoulder_Sleeve_Insignia,_Advanced_Section_Communication_Zone.png","to":"File:Shoulder Sleeve Insignia, Advanced Section Communication Zone.png"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:USA_-_6_ABN_DIV.png","to":"File:USA - 6 ABN DIV.png"},{"fromencoded":false,"from":"File:US_XIV_Corps_patch.svg","to":"File:US XIV Corps patch.svg"}],"pages":[{"pageid":978568,"ns":6,"title":"File:FORCECOM SSI.png","imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"timestamp":"2022-04-16T18:09:21Z","user":"McChizzle","size":168203,"width":198,"height":198,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/FORCECOM_SSI.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1280,"thumbheight":1280,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/FORCECOM_SSI.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1280,"height":1280},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d1/FORCECOM_SSI.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FORCECOM_SSI.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=978568","sha1":"795c460f042025ed197f9f12e7a5be20404fa264","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2022-04-16 18:09:21","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"FORCECOM SSI","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"1941 establishments in the United States|Vector version available|With insignia|PD US Army USAIOH|Blue, red, white|1941 works in the United States|US Army Command Shoulder Sleeve Insignia|Insignia of U.S. Army Forces Command","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Army_Forces_Command\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:United States Army Forces Command\">U.S. Army Forces Command</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoulder_sleeve_insignia_(United_States_Army)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Shoulder sleeve insignia (United States Army)\">Shoulder Sleeve Insignia</a><br><br>\n<b>Design</b>\nA disc 2 1/2 inches diameter consisting of three horizontal stripes of equal width of blue, white and red, the blue uppermost. The insignia has a 1/8 Army green border.<br>\n<b>Symbolism</b>\nThe design was selected during World War I by General Pershing for wear by all personnel assigned to General Headquarters, American Expedition Forces (AEF). The source of the design is a red, white, and blue horizontally striped distinguishing mark worn as a brassard by staff officers when moving about on duty in congested areas in the front of World War I.<br>\n<b>Background</b>\n</p>\nThe shoulder sleeve insignia was originally approved by General Headquarters, AEF, on 7 February 1919; approved by the War Department for GHQ on 17 June 1922; amended to wear the blue uppermost on 19 July 1941; redesignated for Army Ground Forces on 27 March 1942; redesignated for Army Field Forces on 11 May 48; redesignated for Continental Army Command on 9 September 1955; and redesignated for US Army Forces Command effective 1 July 1973.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"16 April 2022 (original upload date)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://tioh.army.mil/Catalog/Heraldry.aspx?HeraldryId=5199&amp;CategoryId=2968&amp;grp=2&amp;menu=Uniformed%20Services&amp;ps=24&amp;p=0\">https://tioh.army.mil/Catalog/Heraldry.aspx?HeraldryId=5199&amp;CategoryId=2968&amp;grp=2&amp;menu=Uniformed%20Services&amp;ps=24&amp;p=0</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<bdi><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/en:United_States_Army_Institute_of_Heraldry\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:en:United States Army Institute of Heraldry\"><span title=\"army organization responsible for furnishing heraldic services to President of the United States and all federal government agencies\">The Institute of Heraldry</span></a></bdi>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"<i>This image shows a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/flag\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:flag\">flag</a>, a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/coat_of_arms\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:coat of arms\">coat of arms</a>, a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/seal_(device)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:seal (device)\">seal</a> or some other official <b><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/insignia\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:insignia\">insignia</a></b> produced by the <b><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Army_Institute_of_Heraldry\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:United States Army Institute of Heraldry\">United States Army Institute of Heraldry</a></b>.  It is in the <b><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/public_domain\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:public domain\">public domain</a></b> but its use is restricted by <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.gpo.gov/fdsys/pkg/USCODE-2010-title18/pdf/USCODE-2010-title18-partI-chap33-sec704.pdf\">Title 18, United States Code, Section 704</a> <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/704\">[1]</a> and the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.gpo.gov/fdsys/granule/CFR-2012-title32-vol3/CFR-2012-title32-vol3-part507/content-detail.html\">Code of Federal Regulations (32 CFR, Part 507)</a> <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/cgi-bin/text-idx?mc=true&amp;node=pt32.3.507&amp;rgn=div5\">[2]</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/32/part-507\">[3]</a>.  Permission to use these images in the USA for most commercial purposes must be obtained from The Institute of Heraldry prior to their use.</i>","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"AttributionRequired":{"value":"false","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Copyrighted":{"value":"False","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value":"insignia","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"License":{"value":"pd","source":"commons-templates","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/png"}],"revisions":[{"revid":1108242822,"parentid":958198356,"timestamp":"2025-11-03T03:18:09Z","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Information\n|Description=[[w:United States Army Forces Command|U.S. Army Forces Command]] [[w:Shoulder sleeve insignia (United States Army)|Shoulder Sleeve Insignia]]<br><br>\n'''Design'''\nA disc 2 1/2 inches diameter consisting of three horizontal stripes of equal width of blue, white and red, the blue uppermost. The insignia has a 1/8 Army green border.<br>\n'''Symbolism'''\nThe design was selected during World War I by General Pershing for wear by all personnel assigned to General Headquarters, American Expedition Forces (AEF). The source of the design is a red, white, and blue horizontally striped distinguishing mark worn as a brassard by staff officers when moving about on duty in congested areas in the front of World War I.<br>\n'''Background'''\nThe shoulder sleeve insignia was originally approved by General Headquarters, AEF, on 7 February 1919; approved by the War Department for GHQ on 17 June 1922; amended to wear the blue uppermost on 19 July 1941; redesignated for Army Ground Forces on 27 March 1942; redesignated for Army Field Forces on 11 May 48; redesignated for Continental Army Command on 9 September 1955; and redesignated for US Army Forces Command effective 1 July 1973.\n|Source=https://tioh.army.mil/Catalog/Heraldry.aspx?HeraldryId=5199&CategoryId=2968&grp=2&menu=Uniformed%20Services&ps=24&p=0\n|Date={{Original upload date|2022-04-16}}\n|Author={{Creator|Wikidata=Q2579238}}\n|Permission= {{PD-USGov-Military-Army-USAIOH}}{{insignia}}\n|other_versions={{vector version available|United States Army Forces Command SSI.svg}}\n}}\n\n[[Category:1941 establishments in the United States|FORSCOM]]\n[[Category:1941 works in the United States|FORSCOM]]\n[[Category:Blue, red, white|FORSCOM]]\n[[Category:Insignia of U.S. Army Forces Command|SSI]]\n[[Category:US Army Command Shoulder Sleeve Insignia|FORSCOM]]"}}}]},{"pageid":5214512,"ns":6,"title":"File:US X Corps SSI.png","imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"timestamp":"2010-07-28T16:10:57Z","user":"Beao","size":361613,"width":1191,"height":1191,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/US_X_Corps_SSI.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1280,"thumbheight":1280,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fc/US_X_Corps_SSI.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1280,"height":1280},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f